  "query_preview": "..Anomalously high moisture continues to reside \nover the region, primarily across the southern CWA where cloud \ncover has generally been blanketed throughout the morning. This \npaired with the exiting of the upper-level shortwave will reduce\ninstability and forcing across the region today, so even with \nthe ample moisture, rainfall is expected to be steadier and more\nstratiform. That being said, CAM\u2019s are still keying in on a few\npotentially stronger cells on the Colorado side of the Four \nCorners. Though stronger storm development would be isolated, \nshould one of these few storms track over a vulnerable area or a\nburn scar, flash flooding will be a possibility, especially \ngiven the saturated nature of soils. \n\nFriday into Saturday, look for flow to dry out and for storm \ncoverage to come to a halt. Some lingering mid-level moisture \ncould maintain partly to mostly cloudy skies, but precipitation \nis not expected for the majority of the region. This quiet \npattern passes quickly though as another surge of moisture is \nanticipated to arrive Sunday night into Monday morning. This is \nbeing driven by strong southwesterly flow from a descending west\ncoast trough drawing tropical moisture from Hurricane Polo into\nthe southwestern CONUS. Confidence is low on timing and \nlocation of impacts though due to diverging model guidance \nbeginning Sunday night. Cluster analysis highlights several \ndifferent portions of the CWA receiving above normal \nprecipitation Sunday through Tuesday, making it difficult to \npick out favored areas for heavy rainfall at this time. This \ncould boil down to how much of a westerly component develops \nfrom the upper-level flow, dictating if the moisture will get \nmostly hung up on the San Juans or if it\u2019ll be able to seep \nfurther into the mountain valleys in west- central Colorado. \n\nIt\u2019s evident that we\u2019ll see another burst of rainier weather \nthrough the early week, but until confidence increases on \nHurricane Polo\u2019s track and the resulting impacts on the \noverarching upper-level pattern, we won\u2019t be able to say much \nother than, \u201cPrepare for a wet start to the week.\u201d",
